    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-2624",
      "problem": "In the figure below, $\\overrightarrow{OC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle AOB.$ If $m \\angle AOD = 90^\\circ$ and $m \\angle COD = 35^\\circ,$ find the measure of $\\angle BOD.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/0b76c6ee6e576fa2701993e941c27f1168c213d9f5f26b1aa8a50759c3711404.png)",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/5ae4873418247e8d825233f903ed359ebee4d2c51ad73e6c7763102a23acded6.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠AOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{35}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOB =2 m \\angle AOC = 110^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, the angle addition postulate gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & =m∠AOB-m∠AOD \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}-{90}^{∘} \\\\  & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
